description:
The target page (/target/<target>, progress.html + progress.js, the
per-port build list) stops updating and only a full page reload brings
it back. Two independent defects, both in progress.js.

1. THE POLL LOOP EXITS AND CANNOT RESTART

  async function pollSummary() {
      try {
          var data = await fetchJSON('summary.json');
          updateSummary(data);          // sets run_active from data.active
          loadNewHistory();
          if (run_active) {
              setTimeout(pollSummary, SbInterval * 1000);
          } else {
              var zone = $('builders_zone_2');
              if (zone) zone.style.display = 'none';
          }
      } catch (e) {
          setTimeout(pollSummary, SbInterval * 500);
      }
  }

It reschedules only while run_active. When a build finishes -- or when
the page is opened BETWEEN builds -- the loop exits and nothing ever
restarts it. The next build is never picked up. This is the "frozen"
case and why F5 always fixes it.

Note the catch path retries at SbInterval * 500, i.e. twice as FAST on
failure. Backwards: a failing backend gets hit harder.

2. THE PORT LIST FREEZES WHILE THE COUNTERS KEEP MOVING

  var historyRunning = false;
  async function loadNewHistory() {
      if (historyRunning) return;
      historyRunning = true;
      ...
      var results = await Promise.all(fetches);
      for (...) { addHistoryData(results[i]); ... }   // unguarded
      historyRunning = false;
  }

No try/finally. The individual fetches are .catch'd to null, but
addHistoryData() is not. One throw leaves historyRunning stuck true,
and because pollSummary calls it fire-and-forget (no await, no .catch)
the rejection is silent. Every later call then returns at the guard:
summary counters keep updating while the port list never grows again.
This is the "badly updated" case, and it looks intermittent because it
needs a malformed or partial NN_history.json to trigger.

3. F5 IS SWALLOWED

  document.addEventListener('keydown', function(e) {
      if (e.key === 'F5') e.preventDefault();
  });

The page blocks the one recovery that works. Ctrl-R still gets through.

FIX: ADOPT dpLive FOR THE LOOP

static/live.js already exists for exactly this -- "one resilient polling
loop for every live surface ... this replaces the per-page hand-rolled
setTimeout/setInterval pollers so there is one cadence/backoff/lifecycle
to reason about and test". Used today by index.html, agentic-job.js,
agentic-bundle.js and agentic-runner.js.

It fixes (1) structurally: dpLive owns scheduling and reschedules unless
onData returns "stop", so progress just never returns "stop" and an idle
page keeps ticking and picks the next build up. It also brings
pauseWhenHidden -- progress currently polls at full rate in a background
tab for the whole length of a build -- and a try/catch around
fetch+onData that always reschedules, replacing the backwards fast-retry.

It does NOT fix (2). loadNewHistory is fire-and-forget, so its rejection
is a separate promise chain that dpLive's try/catch never sees: onData
returns before that async function settles. The try/finally is still
required.

WHAT THIS USE CASE EXPOSES IN dpLive

Backoff is monotonic and never resets:

  if (backoffStep && interval < maxInterval) {
      interval = Math.min(interval + backoffStep, maxInterval);
  }

Nothing lowers interval again. Fine for the current users -- the bundle
verify-wait is waiting for one terminal event -- but wrong for a page
that idles between builds and must become responsive when one starts:
it would sit at maxIntervalMs at exactly the wrong moment. poke()
schedules an immediate tick but does not reset the interval.

So adopting dpLive here wants either a resetBackoff() on the handle, or
onData resetting when it sees data.active flip 0 -> 1. Small addition,
but it should land with this rather than after, or the slow-poll config
cannot be used and the page has to stay on a fixed fast cadence.
